.TH CHMAP_GET_ELEM_REF 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chmap_get_elem_ref \- get reference to value by key
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chashmap.h>

ccol_retval_t chmap_get_elem_ref(chmap chm,
                                  const cmap_pair *key_pair,
                                  const cmap_pair **val_pair);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Gets a reference to the value that belongs to the given key. The
reference points at internal storage. The caller can read the value
through it, and can change the bytes of the value in place.

The out-parameter is a const cmap_pair **. The caller's own variable
must therefore be a const cmap_pair *. The address of a plain
cmap_pair * does not compile.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
chm        Hash map to search
key_pair   Key to look up
val_pair   Address of a const cmap_pair * to receive the reference
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success        - Key found, *val_pair points to value
ccol_invalid_args   - chm, key_pair, or val_pair is NULL or key size is 0;
                      also returned when key_pair->size does not exactly
                      match the byte size of the key type, for any
                      fixed-width numeric key type (char, short, int, long,
                      long long, their unsigned counterparts, float,
                      double, long double, or a pointer type), regardless
                      of which backend the map uses
ccol_key_not_found  - Key does not exist in map
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The ptr of a key_pair needs no specified alignment. The map hashes and
compares the key bytes through memcpy. It does not use a pointer cast to
the key type. key_pair->ptr can therefore point directly at key bytes at
any offset in a larger buffer. Examples are a field in a packed network
frame and a memory-mapped record. You do not have to copy the key bytes
into an aligned local variable first
The cmap_pair is the map's own accessor for that entry. It describes
the value. It is not a part of the value. Its target is const. The
caller can therefore read through val_pair->ptr. The caller can also
write to the bytes that it points at, inside val_pair->size. But an
assignment to val_pair->ptr or to val_pair->size itself is a compile
error. The two fields describe one another. The map also cannot own
a pointer that it did not allocate. Use chmap_insert_elem(3) to
replace a value
Average O(1) complexity
The reference stays valid until the next insert, delete or resize. A
lookup for a different key never invalidates it. The caller can hold
references for different keys at the same time
val_pair->ptr is correctly aligned for any value type. This is true
for both internal backends
For a float or double key, see chmap_construct(3) for the map's
key equality contract (bitwise, except -0.0 canonicalized to 0.0)
For a long double key, see chmap_construct(3) for the map's key
equality contract (value-based, unlike every other key type)
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chmap_get (3),
.BR chmap_get_ptr (3),
.BR chmap_insert_elem (3),
.BR chmap_construct (3)
